# Pokedex — Checkpoint 12.2: Pokédex UX polish, variant-aware navigation and denser detail

## Assignment
- Target agent: coding executor
- Mode: BOUNDED_CORRECTION
- Boundary: CHECKPOINT
- Risk: MEDIUM
- Repository: /home/thib/Documents/Lab/Pokedex
- Branch: main
- Expected starting HEAD: 5684fdbeee9ba246b71b2965a830b93914e98518
- File authority: MODIFY_IN_SCOPE
- Command authority: RUN_REPOSITORY_CHECKS
- Git authority: COMMIT_AND_PUSH_AFTER_REQUIRED_CHECKS
- Network authority: SPECIFIC_ALLOWLIST: docs.expo.dev, pokeapi.co, GitHub

## Verified starting state
Checkpoints 01–12.1 are accepted.

Remaining concrete UX defects observed during manual use:
- web TextInput shows an unwanted purple/native browser focus outline;
- finding a late-Dex Pokémon such as Ho-Oh requires repeatedly pressing "Charger plus";
- type filtering has the same partial-data problem;
- the horizontal type-chip strip is not discoverable and overflows off-screen;
- a Shiny favorite shown on Home/Collection opens its detail screen as Normal;
- the Pokémon detail card feels visually sparse and Base Stats are undersized.

## Objective
Fix these bounded UX issues without starting CP13 i18n and without violating the local-search requirement.

## Acceptance criteria

1. Remove the browser-native purple focus outline from the Pokédex search input on web.

2. Do NOT simply suppress keyboard focus visibility.
   Replace it with a deliberate app-owned focused state on the search field/wrapper so keyboard/mouse focus remains visually perceivable.

3. Replace manual normal pagination with sequential automatic background hydration:
   - first page remains 30;
   - continue loading generic 30-item pages until #251;
   - maximum one page in flight;
   - never launch all pages concurrently;
   - stop cleanly on unmount or error;
   - expose progress/loading/error/retry in the footer;
   - normal usage must no longer require repeatedly pressing "Charger plus".

4. Preserve the teacher constraint:
   - search still filters only `items` already loaded in client memory;
   - filter still filters only loaded client data;
   - never call `/pokemon/{typedQuery}`;
   - never send the search query or selected type to PokéAPI;
   - never use `/type/{type}` for remote filtering.
   Auto-pagination is generic and independent of the entered query/filter.

5. As automatic pages arrive, an active query/filter must recompute naturally.
   Example: searching `ho-oh` early may initially show no result, but Ho-Oh must appear automatically once #250 has been loaded, without another user action.

6. Replace the horizontal scrolling type-chip row with one compact Filter control in the Pokédex header.

7. Opening the Filter control shows a clear selector/panel:
   - `Tous` first and selected by default;
   - all 18 supported types available regardless of current loaded pages;
   - types sorted alphabetically for now;
   - type color indicator retained;
   - selecting one closes the selector and applies the existing local type filter;
   - current filter is visually understandable from the closed control.
   CP13 will translate these labels; do not implement i18n here.

8. Preserve the existing ID/name sort controls, 3-column grid and all accepted search/filter semantics.

9. Make favorite-variant navigation contextual:
   - Home hero navigation passes its displayed `normal|shiny` variant;
   - Collection slots pass their stored variant;
   - `/pokemon/[id]` accepts an optional route variant;
   - arriving with `variant=shiny` opens the Shiny presentation immediately when available;
   - arriving from Pokédex without a variant still opens Normal;
   - invalid/unavailable shiny gracefully falls back to Normal.
   Do not globally force stored favorite variants when entering from the ordinary Pokédex list.

10. Existing Normal/Shiny controls on detail remain local and functional after opening.

11. Increase information density/readability of the detail card:
   - make Base Stats visibly larger;
   - approximately 12px labels/values rather than the current 10px;
   - thicker ~6px stat tracks;
   - taller rows / stronger vertical rhythm;
   - section heading may increase modestly;
   - preserve 3-digit values and Reanimated fill behavior.

12. Reduce the excessive empty feeling in the detail card:
   - avoid very large artificial gaps between actions, About and Base Stats;
   - retain clean spacing and the white content surface;
   - verify both ~360×640 and ~393×850 so the short layout does not overflow while the tall layout no longer feels hollow.

13. Preserve all CP12.1 favorite-v2 behavior, Home selection, Collection insertion order, cries, previous/next navigation and all existing data contracts.

14. Append exactly one `IMP-012B` entry to `docs/ai-usage.jsonl`; preserve all previous entries byte-for-byte.

## Implementation notes
For the filter selector, prefer existing React Native/Expo primitives. A lightweight Modal/popover-style surface is sufficient; do not add a UI framework.

Derive the 18 filter options from the existing canonical type/color source instead of duplicating another type list.

For auto-hydration, keep the existing `fetchPokemonBatch` API and mergeUnique logic. This checkpoint must not redesign the PokéAPI layer.

For variant navigation, prefer an optional route param such as:
`/pokemon/[id]?variant=shiny`
rather than global state.

## Explicitly out of scope
Global i18n, translated type names, localized abilities, accessibility/WCAG audit, splash/icon, dark mode, caching architecture, additional generations.

## Validation
Run:
- npm run lint
- npx tsc --noEmit
- npx expo export --platform android
- npx expo export --platform web
- npx expo-doctor

Explicit runtime evidence:
- focus search on web: no purple browser outline; custom focus visible;
- type `ho-oh` before #250 is loaded and prove it appears automatically later;
- select a late-generation type case and prove new matching results appear as pages hydrate;
- prove query/type values never appear in network URLs;
- all 251 eventually load through sequential paging;
- Filter panel contains Tous + exactly 18 alphabetically ordered types;
- shiny Home Noctali → detail opens shiny;
- shiny Collection entry → detail opens shiny;
- normal Pokédex card → detail opens normal;
- detail stats animation still runs and is visually larger;
- inspect detail at ~360×640 and ~393×850.

## Git completion policy
Stage only task-owned changes, commit on main and push origin/main.
No force-push, rebase, history rewrite or PR mutation.

## Required final report
Status; starting ref; AC1–AC14; changed files; auto-pagination/network evidence; local-search guarantee; filter UX evidence; focus-fix evidence; variant-navigation evidence; detail-density measurements; web/native visual-proof status; git status; commit SHA + push result.

# Pokedex — Checkpoint 13: global FR/EN i18n and localized Pokémon data

## Assignment
- Target agent: coding executor
- Mode: IMPLEMENTATION
- Boundary: CHECKPOINT
- Risk: MEDIUM
- Repository: /home/thib/Documents/Lab/Pokedex
- Branch: main
- Expected starting HEAD: 4972c657235b9cb4f4fd34f1c87d4faa181f627d
- File authority: MODIFY_IN_SCOPE
- Command authority: RUN_REPOSITORY_CHECKS
- Git authority: COMMIT_AND_PUSH_AFTER_REQUIRED_CHECKS
- Network authority: SPECIFIC_ALLOWLIST: docs.expo.dev, pokeapi.co, www.i18next.com, react.i18next.com, registry.npmjs.org, GitHub
- Secrets / external systems authority: NONE

## Verified starting state
Checkpoints 01–12.2 are accepted.

The application currently has:
- a unified Pokédex design system;
- Pokémon names already normalized as `{ fr, en }`;
- a detail-local FR/EN name toggle;
- one FR-preferred description only;
- raw canonical type slugs such as `water`, `dark`, `grass`;
- raw ability slugs such as `inner-focus`, humanized only for display;
- user-facing strings hardcoded across routes/components;
- AsyncStorage already available;
- Favorites variant persistence and all accepted Pokédex behavior.

Current accepted constraints MUST remain:
- search is local only;
- auto-hydration is generic and independent of query/filter;
- National Dex scope remains #001–#251;
- Normal/Shiny remains independent of language.

## Objective
Implement a real global French/English localization system.

Changing language must update the entire visible application consistently:
- application UI;
- Pokémon displayed names;
- type labels;
- species descriptions;
- ability/talent names;
- relevant accessibility labels;
- locale-sensitive number formatting.

Language switching must be local after data is loaded and must not refetch Pokémon merely because the locale changed.

## Acceptance criteria

### Global i18n foundation

1. Install the smallest appropriate localization stack using repository conventions, preferably:
   - `i18next`
   - `react-i18next`
   - `expo-localization`
   using `npx expo install ...`.

2. Add a focused i18n module/resources outside route code.
   French and English resources must have matching key structures.

3. Supported application languages are exactly:
   - `fr`
   - `en`

4. Initial language resolution:
   - use a valid persisted user choice first;
   - otherwise use the device locale when it resolves to `fr` or `en`;
   - otherwise fall back to French.

5. Persist manual language choice in AsyncStorage under a versioned key.
   Invalid/corrupt stored values must degrade safely.

6. Prevent a visible wrong-language flash during startup.
   Coordinate localization hydration with the existing font/splash bootstrap rather than briefly rendering French and then switching to English.

### Global language control

7. Replace the detail-local FR/EN segmented control with one GLOBAL language control.

8. The global language control must be reachable from every primary screen through the shared Pokédex shell/header without destroying existing trailing metadata such as:
   - `#197`;
   - Collection favorite count.

9. Switching language updates the current screen immediately without navigation or restart.

10. Language switching itself must issue zero PokéAPI requests.

### UI string localization

11. Move user-facing application copy into i18n resources rather than scattering language conditionals through components.

Cover at minimum:
- Home;
- Pokédex;
- Collection;
- Pokémon detail;
- search placeholder;
- sort/filter UI;
- loading/empty/error/retry states;
- favorite counts;
- buttons/actions;
- About labels;
- Base Stats heading/labels where language-specific;
- accessibility labels and hints;
- cry/favorite controls where applicable.

Do not mechanically translate canonical technical values used for logic.

12. Remove obsolete hardcoded user-facing FR/EN switching logic after migration.
   Avoid constructs such as:
   `language === 'fr' ? '...' : '...'`
   throughout route components when an i18n key should be used instead.

### Pokémon names and search/sort

13. Pokémon display names follow the global language:
   - #197 → `Noctali` in FR;
   - #197 → `Umbreon` in EN.

14. PokemonCard, Home, Collection and detail all use the global language for displayed Pokémon names.

15. Search remains language-friendly and LOCAL:
   it must continue matching BOTH French and English Pokémon names regardless of the currently displayed language.
   Example: searching `Noctali` while the UI is English may still find Umbreon.

16. Alphabetical name sorting follows the currently displayed language:
   - French UI sorts on `names.fr` with an appropriate French locale;
   - English UI sorts on `names.en` with an appropriate English locale.
   Dex sorting remains unchanged.

### Type localization

17. Keep canonical type slugs (`water`, `dark`, etc.) in the domain/API model and filtering logic.

18. Translate all 18 type labels through i18n resources.
   Examples:
   - `water` → `Eau` / `Water`
   - `dark` → `Ténèbres` / `Dark`
   - `grass` → `Plante` / `Grass`
   - `fire` → `Feu` / `Fire`

19. TypeBadge uses the localized label while keeping the canonical slug for color lookup.

20. The Pokédex type-filter panel:
   - contains the same canonical 18 types;
   - displays translated labels;
   - sorts type options alphabetically by their DISPLAYED localized label;
   - preserves canonical type filtering internally.

### Localized descriptions

21. Replace the single FR-preferred detail description with both localized values in the normalized domain model, e.g. conceptually:
   `descriptions: { fr: string | null; en: string | null }`.

22. Extract the latest usable PokéAPI flavor text independently for FR and EN and normalize whitespace as today.

23. Detail description follows the global language.
   If the selected language is unavailable, fall back gracefully to the other supported language, then to the translated “description unavailable” UI string.

### Localized abilities / talents

24. Replace raw-display ability slugs with localized ability names sourced from PokéAPI `/ability/...`.

25. Normalize each ability to retain:
   - canonical API name;
   - localized FR name;
   - localized EN name;
   - `isHidden`.

26. Fetch both localized names up front when loading Pokémon details, so changing global language after the detail has loaded causes ZERO additional ability/Pokémon requests.

27. Add a small per-session ability localization cache if useful so revisiting Pokémon sharing an ability does not repeatedly request the same ability resource.

28. Provide safe fallbacks if PokéAPI lacks a localized ability name:
   localized other-language name → humanized canonical slug.

29. Translate the hidden-ability marker:
   - FR: appropriate French wording;
   - EN: appropriate English wording.

### Locale-sensitive detail formatting

30. Weight/height formatting follows the active locale:
   - FR example: `27,0 kg`, `1,0 m`
   - EN example: `27.0 kg`, `1.0 m`
   Keep metric units in both languages.

31. Stat labels are localized where appropriate while preserving the same six-stat order and existing Reanimated behavior.

### Existing behavior preservation

32. Preserve:
   - auto-hydrating #001–#251 Pokédex;
   - local-only search/filter;
   - sort;
   - filter modal;
   - 3-column grid;
   - Normal/Shiny;
   - variant-aware Home/Collection navigation;
   - cries;
   - favorites v2;
   - Home hero selection;
   - Collection insertion order;
   - previous/next Pokémon;
   - stat animation;
   - unified design system;
   - web AppShell.

33. Do not change Favorites storage merely because language changes.
   Persist canonical id + sprite variant only.

34. Do not persist translated Pokémon data or PokéAPI snapshots into AsyncStorage.

35. Append exactly one `IMP-013` entry to `docs/ai-usage.jsonl`; preserve all previous entries byte-for-byte.

## Implementation guidance

Prefer a structure conceptually similar to:

src/
├── i18n/
│   ├── index.ts
│   ├── languageStorage.ts
│   └── locales/
│       ├── fr.ts
│       └── en.ts

Exact names are not prescribed.

Keep canonical domain values language-neutral:
- type = `dark`
- ability apiName = `synchronize`
- Pokémon apiName = `umbreon`

Localization belongs at normalization/display boundaries, not in routing/filter identifiers.

Do not put a large translation dictionary inside route files.

The global language selector should reuse the shared shell rather than reintroducing route-specific language controls.

## Explicitly out of scope
Accessibility/WCAG audit, splash/icon redesign, dark mode, new generations, API-wide caching, new product features, design overhaul.

## Preflight and safety
Read AGENTS.md/CLAUDE.md.
Verify exact HEAD, upstream and clean worktree.

Inspect at minimum:
- package.json;
- root layout/bootstrap;
- PokedexShell;
- all four routes;
- PokemonCard;
- TypeBadge;
- CryButton;
- pokemonList utilities;
- pokeApi normalization/types;
- Favorites storage/provider.

Read the current Expo SDK 57 localization documentation before using Expo localization APIs.

Stop on unexpected ref or unowned changes.

## Validation

Run:
- npm run lint
- npx tsc --noEmit
- npx expo export --platform android
- npx expo export --platform web
- npx expo-doctor

Also perform structural/runtime verification.

### Resource integrity
- FR and EN translation trees contain the same keys.
- Type resource contains exactly all 18 canonical types in both languages.
- no missing-key warnings during normal navigation.

### Persistence
- first run on FR device → FR;
- first run on EN device → EN;
- unsupported device locale → FR;
- select EN → reload/restart → EN remains;
- corrupt persisted locale → safe fallback.

### Global switching
From each of:
- Home;
- Pokédex;
- Collection;
- Detail;

switch FR ↔ EN and prove visible UI updates immediately.

### Pokémon localization
Verify at least:
- #001 Bulbizarre ↔ Bulbasaur;
- #197 Noctali ↔ Umbreon;
- #250 Ho-Oh remains appropriate in both languages.

### Type localization
Verify examples and all 18 keys.
Filter on a translated label but prove the stored/filter value remains canonical.

### Search
With UI in EN:
- searching `Noctali` finds Umbreon.
With UI in FR:
- searching `Umbreon` finds Noctali.
Both must cause zero query-driven network requests.

### Sorting
Prove name sorting differs according to the displayed-language name field and never mutates canonical loaded data.

### Detail
Verify one Pokémon with:
- localized name;
- localized description;
- localized type;
- localized abilities;
- translated hidden marker;
- localized About/Base Stats strings;
- decimal comma FR / decimal point EN.

#197 is a preferred end-to-end case.

### Network behavior
After a detail is completely loaded:
- switch FR → EN → FR;
- prove zero additional Pokémon/species/ability requests are caused by language switching.

Report the additional detail-load request cost introduced by ability localization and any cache hits.

### Regression checks
- Shiny favorite still opens Shiny.
- Pokédex card still opens Normal.
- cry still works structurally.
- favorites are unchanged by locale switch.
- type filtering remains local.
- all 251 still hydrate sequentially.

## Git completion policy
Review task-owned diff.
Stage only task-owned files.
Commit on main and push origin/main.

No force-push, rebase, history rewrite or PR mutation.

## Required final report
Status; starting ref; AC1–AC35 PASS/FAIL/NOT PROVEN; dependencies added; changed files; i18n architecture; language-resolution/persistence evidence; translation-resource integrity; Pokémon/type/description/ability localization evidence; search/sort evidence; zero-request language-switch evidence; ability request/cache evidence; preserved-feature evidence; web/native visual-proof status; final git status; commit SHA + push result; remaining issues before the next checkpoint.
